2026-09-07T03:54:19.7292351Z   PR_BODY: Closes #1079.

Four CI/CD gates in this repository were configured but not enforced. In every
one of the four cases the unenforced state was byte-identical to the enforced
one — the job was green, the step was present, the policy file said the right
thing, and nothing was actually being checked. That is the shape of the whole
pull request: each fix is small, and each is paired with a unit test that fails
when the gate goes quiet again.

A fifth gate had the opposite failure. `Self-Hosting Evidence Check` reported
something true about this branch and left no way to answer it, because the
remedy it implies — amend the commit — is forbidden by the repository's own
ruleset. Fixing that surfaced a sixth defect in the same check.

The reconstruction, the raw logs, the measurements and the upstream reports are
in [`dev/log/issues/1079/pulls/1080/`](dev/log/issues/1079/pulls/1080/README.md).

## The defect register

All ten workflow runs at head `f971b8205` were downloaded with their logs and
23 annotations before anything was changed. Seven defects came out of it; two
more (D8, D9) came out of this pull request's own CI and are kept in the same
register, because a defect found by running the fix is still a defect.

| | Defect | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **D1** | `Auto Release` blocks: the release cycle contains no merged Formal-AI-authored pull request | **true positive, deliberately not "fixed"** — the gate is reporting this repository's actual state, and making it green would mean deleting the gate |
| **D2** | `Security / Rust dependency audit` printed two findings and exited **0** | fixed |
| **D3** | `.github/zizmor.yml`'s `'*': hash-pin` policy had never been applied to a container image | fixed |
| **D4** | `check-file-size` warnings on four files | working as designed — warnings, not errors, and not growing |
| **D5** | `learning-cycle` notice annotations | working as designed |
| **D6** | The zizmor step documented reproducing with a version CI cannot install | fixed |
| **D7** | 46 of 48 `actions/checkout` steps persisted the job token into `.git/config` | fixed |
| **D8** | `Self-Hosting Evidence Check` reports a correct finding whose only remedy — amending the commit — the repository's ruleset forbids on every branch | fixed |
| **D9** | The same check read an *indented* `Formal-AI-*:` line as a declared trailer, so documenting a trailer declared one | fixed |

### D2 — a green gate over two real findings

`cargo audit` classifies `unmaintained`, `unsound` and `yanked` as *warnings*,
and a warning does not move its exit status. The run ended
`warning: 2 allowed warnings found` and exited 0 — with `Cargo.lock` pinning
`chacha20 0.10.1`, a release its own authors had yanked.

`scripts/check-rust-dependencies.sh` now passes `--deny warnings`, and both
findings are answered rather than suppressed. `chacha20` is upgraded past the
yank. `fxhash` is genuinely compiled in through
`web-capture → scraper 0.21 → selectors 0.26` and carries `patched = []`, so no
lockfile edit clears it; it is ignored under a new proof form, `blocked-upstream`,
which expires in the opposite direction from the existing `unreachable` form:
it fails the moment the crate *leaves* the build graph, which is exactly when
the upstream fix lands, and it must name the report it is waiting on.

### D3 — a policy that could not reach what it was written for

`'*': hash-pin` has been in `.github/zizmor.yml` since #1076. It configures the
`unpinned-uses` audit, which reads *action* references. Container images belong
to the separate `unpinned-images` audit, which zizmor classifies as **Pedantic**
while the job ran `--persona regular`. So `docker://rhysd/actionlint:1.7.12` — a
mutable third-party tag executing with the repository checked out — sat inside
the workflow whose purpose is auditing the pipeline.

Both actionlint references are now digest-pinned, and a second zizmor pass runs
`--persona pedantic --min-severity high --min-confidence high`: **0** of the 164
pedantic findings on the clean tree, **2** with the protections reverted. There
is no narrower expression available — zizmor 1.29 and 1.30 both reject
`rules.<audit>.persona`, and `remap` rewrites only severity, which is not what
the persona filter reads.

### D6 — a pin that freezes instead of floating

`zizmor-action` resolves versions from a static 37-row table shipped inside the
action and `die`s on anything absent. v0.6.2's `latest` row carries the same
digest as its `1.29.0` row, so the default does not float — it freezes one minor
release behind, while the step comment told maintainers to reproduce with
`zizmor==1.30.0`. Both passes now set `version: 1.29.0` explicitly.

### D7 — the finding no configured gate could report

46 of this repository's 48 `actions/checkout` steps left the job token in
`.git/config` as an `http.extraheader`, readable by every later step and by
anything they shell out to. zizmor reports this as `artipacked` at **Low**
confidence — below both of this repository's gates (medium on the default pass,
high on the pedantic one), so no configured check was ever going to report it.
All five templates set `persist-credentials: false`.

42 checkouts across 18 workflow files now drop the credential, joining the two
that already did, for **44 of 48**. The four that keep it are the four jobs that
push with it: `external-benchmarks.yml` records scheduled upstream results with
`git push`; `release.yml`'s `auto-release` and `manual-release` publish the
version bump and its tag through `scripts/version-and-commit.rs`; and its
`changelog-pr` job pushes a branch through `peter-evans/create-pull-request`.
Each carries a comment above the step naming the issue and the push, and the
test requires that comment before it will accept an exception.

Measured as **46 `artipacked` findings → 4**, the four being exactly those
sites, with every other audit count unchanged (162 → 118 total). Transcript:
[`analysis/artipacked-sweep.md`](dev/log/issues/1079/pulls/1080/analysis/artipacked-sweep.md).

**The sweep is checked in both directions, because the first pass of it got the
expensive direction wrong.** It initially removed the credential from
`auto-release` and `manual-release`, whose release push authenticates with
exactly that credential — `scripts/git-config.rs` sets the committer identity
and no credential helper. Both jobs run only on `main` after a merge, so no
pull request would have caught it; it would have surfaced as a release that
publishes to crates.io and GHCR and then fails at `git push` with the version
bump uncommitted. The fix is a second test rather than a note:
`every_job_that_pushes_still_has_a_credential_to_push_with` reads each job body
for a remote git write and fails if such a job sets `persist-credentials: false`.
`analysis/artipacked-sweep.md` records what the first scan missed and why.

### D8 — a correct finding with no available remedy

`Self-Hosting Evidence Check` failed on this pull request at `ef3bb4aff`:
commit `bd511432a` records `Formal-AI-Evidence` without `Formal-AI-Session`.
The finding is right and the commit is mine.

The remedy it implies does not exist here. Ruleset 21300712 applies `deletion`
and `non_fast_forward` to **`~ALL`** branches with an empty `bypass_actors`; a
`filter-branch` history that passed the metric locally was rejected on push
with `GH013 … Cannot force-push to this branch`. A gate keyed to commit-message
content, in a repository where a pushed commit message is permanent, is a gate
whose only escape is to abandon the branch — the same deadlock shape
#796/#810/#812 already removed from the *release* path, surviving on the
pull-request path only because nobody had mis-trailered a commit there yet.

A later commit in the same measured range now withdraws the earlier claim with
`Formal-AI-Retract: <full 40-character sha>`. It is **one-directional by
construction**: a retraction only moves a commit *out* of the numerator, and no
trailer moves one in without the session evidence that was always required, so
no retraction can raise the measured share. The target must be a full
40-character sha inside the range being measured and may not be the commit
carrying it; a malformed retraction errors under `Strict` and warns under
`Lenient`, so it cannot deadlock a release either. It is applied on **both**
walks — `measure_with_policy` and `merged_self_authored_pull_requests` —
because applying it on one only would let a commit leave the measured share and
still count toward the release floor.

This pull request retracts two commits and claims no attribution of its own.
Transcript, including the ruleset query and the rejected push:
[`analysis/self-hosting-evidence-deadlock.md`](dev/log/issues/1079/pulls/1080/analysis/self-hosting-evidence-deadlock.md).

### D9 — documenting a trailer declared one

The commit introducing the retraction showed the trailer's format in an
indented example in its own message, and the check answered
`must name a full 40-character sha, found <full 40-character sha>`.
`trailer_values` scans the whole commit body rather than using git's
`%(trailers)` placeholder — issue #796, where a blank line between two trailers
hid one of them — and it trimmed each line before matching the key.
`git interpret-trailers --parse` returns nothing for an indented line; so does
this parser now, with #796's blank-line tolerance untouched.

## How to reproduce each defect, and the test that pins it

Every one of the nine tests in `tests/unit/ci-cd/issue_1079.rs` fails without
Docker, without network and without a CI run — because each defect was a gate whose broken state
was byte-identical to its working one, so a test that needs the pipeline to run
would not have caught it either.

| Reproduce | Test |
|---|---|
| revert `--deny warnings`; `cargo audit` prints the findings and exits 0 | `the_rust_dependency_audit_treats_a_warning_as_a_finding` |
| restore the tag in place of the digest; the default zizmor pass still reports nothing | `every_container_image_is_digest_pinned_or_explicitly_excepted`, `a_pedantic_pass_enforces_the_hash_pin_policy_on_images` |
| remove `version: 1.29.0`; the action installs 1.29.0 anyway, not the documented 1.30.0 | `every_zizmor_pass_pins_the_version_its_comment_documents` |
| drop `persist-credentials: false` from any checkout; both configured gates stay green | `every_checkout_drops_its_credential_unless_it_pushes` |
| add it to a job that pushes; nothing fails until the next release runs on `main` | `every_job_that_pushes_still_has_a_credential_to_push_with` |
| ignore an advisory with no proof line, or with both proof forms at once | `every_ignored_advisory_carries_exactly_one_proof` |
| half-trailer a commit on a branch that cannot be force-pushed | `self_hosting_metric::a_retraction_unblocks_a_branch_whose_history_cannot_be_rewritten` (+ three more: the share can only fall, the release floor moves with it, a short or out-of-range sha is an error) |
| write an indented `Formal-AI-Retract:` example in a commit message | `self_hosting_metric::an_indented_example_of_a_trailer_is_not_a_trailer` |

The last two sweep `.github/` from the directory rather than a hand-written
list: a defect that only holds for the files someone remembered to enumerate is
not an invariant, and every defect here was originally introduced by a file
nobody thought to look at.

## Requirement 2 — comparison against all five templates

All five `link-foundation/*-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template` trees were
snapshotted at immutable commits and compared file by file, in both directions.
The full matrix is in the evidence bundle. The one practice this repository had
not adopted was `persist-credentials: false` (D7), now adopted.

## Requirement 3 — upstream reports

Six reports filed across six repositories, thirteen issue URLs, each with a
reproduction that runs at the snapshotted commit, a workaround, and the
code-level fix:

- **cargo-audit exits 0 on warnings** — [rust#164](https://github.com/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/164)
- **hash-pin policy cannot reach container images** — [rust#165](https://github.com/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/165), [js#177](https://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/177), [python#75](https://github.com/link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/75), [php#5](https://github.com/link-foundation/php-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/5), [csharp#52](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/52)
- **documented zizmor version is uninstallable** — [rust#166](https://github.com/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/166), [js#178](https://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/178), [python#76](https://github.com/link-foundation/python-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/76), [php#6](https://github.com/link-foundation/php-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/6)
- **csharp template has no zizmor at all** (4 high-severity `template-injection`, 2 `excessive-permissions` when the other templates' invocation is run on it) — [csharp#53](https://github.com/link-foundation/csharp-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/53)
- **php template has no `security.yml` at all** — [php#7](https://github.com/link-foundation/php-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/7)
- **`scraper 0.25` clears RUSTSEC-2025-0057 with no source changes** — [link-assistant/web-capture#155](https://github.com/link-assistant/web-capture/issues/155)

## Verbose output

`FORMAL_AI_CI_VERBOSE` already exists and stays off by default; §7 of the
evidence README records what it covers and where.

